        "The story is often told as a lesson about the importance of evidence. But it is also a lesson about how difficult it can be for people to accept that their own habits are harmful. Even today, studies in many countries find that health workers do not always wash their hands as often as guidelines ({b:43}), especially when they are busy. Hospitals have tried many approaches: placing hand gel next to every bed, giving staff regular feedback, and encouraging patients to remind their doctors. {u:⑶:The most effective programs tend to combine several of these measures rather than relying on any single one.}",
        "Hand hygiene has also become a central part of public health outside hospitals. During outbreaks of infectious disease, campaigns ( Ｃ ) people to wash their hands with soap for at least twenty seconds have helped reduce the spread of illness. In regions where clean water is ( Ｄ ), simply providing soap and a place to wash can lower the number of children who fall ill. A practice that was once ({b:44}) by experts is now taught to young children everywhere — a reminder that the most powerful medical advances are not always the most complicated, and that {u:⑷:changing minds} can be as ({b:45}) as making the discovery itself.",
